<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:64:default:gcc">
<!--
    kuna decodehalt (DIV-151): a RETURN kuna planted because it could NOT
    decode the bytes says so, instead of printing as an ordinary `return;`.

    FlowInfo ends a path it cannot follow with an ARTIFICIAL HALT - a
    CPUI_RETURN carrying the constant 1 and one of the getHaltType() bits.
    Three of those bits are decode failures rather than program returns:
    badinstruction, unimplemented and missing.  Upstream PrintC::opReturn
    (printc.cc:774) renders them as the pseudo-calls halt_baddata() /
    halt_unimplemented() / halt_missing(), and processInstruction buffers
    "Bad instruction - Truncating control flow here" at the failing address
    plus a header warning on the function.  kuna ported the halt but neither
    the rendering nor the warnings, so a function that ran off into bytes it
    could not read ended with a bare `return;` and nothing said so.

    Stage model: P2 lift / flow-classification.  P0 assertion:
    `option decodehalt on|off`.

    dh_fn (0x100000): test edi,edi / je +1 / ret / .byte 0x06
      0x06 (PUSH ES) is not an instruction in 64-bit mode, so the je target
      at 0x100005 is undecodable and the flow follower halts there.

    `option voidtailreturn on` is set for both passes because that is what
    `kuna decompile` runs (mode auto -> aggressive), and it is the seam this
    feature has to survive: voidtailreturn elides a void function's trailing
    bare `return;`, and the artificial halt has exactly that shape, so without
    the halt-type exclusion in elidable_void_tail_return the truncation marker
    is deleted and its warning lands on whatever statement decoded before it.

    Pass 1 (option decodehalt off, the pre-fix behaviour): the truncated path
    ends in NOTHING - the halt is a bare `return;` and voidtailreturn elides
    it - so the body reads as a fully analysed function with a clean early
    exit, and the prototype carries no warning (assert #1, a 5-line chain,
    exactly once).  The option is set BEFORE `lo fu` on purpose: the comment
    database accumulates across passes, so a warning buffered by an earlier
    flow-follow would still be printed here.

    Pass 2 (option decodehalt on, the shipped default): the same body ends
    halt_baddata() carrying the truncation warning (assert #2) under a
    prototype carrying the header warning (assert #3), and the pseudo-call
    appears exactly once over both passes (assert #4), so it is pass 2 that
    produced it.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
85ff7401c306
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="dh_fn"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <com>parse line extern void dh_fn(int x);</com>
  <com>option voidtailreturn on</com>
  <com>option decodehalt off</com>
  <com>lo fu dh_fn</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>option decodehalt on</com>
  <com>lo fu dh_fn</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
<stringmatch name="DECODEHALT #1: off, the undecodable path leaves no statement at all and the prototype says nothing" min="1" max="1">^void dh_fn\(int4 x\)$
^\{$
^  if \(x\)$
^    return;$
^\}$</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="DECODEHALT #2: on, the halt names the decode failure and carries the truncation warning" min="1" max="1">^  halt_baddata\(\); // warn: Bad instruction - Truncating control flow here$</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="DECODEHALT #3: on, the function header says the body is incomplete" min="1" max="1">^void dh_fn\(int4 x\) // warn: Control flow encountered bad instruction data$</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="DECODEHALT #4: exactly one of the two passes renders the pseudo-call" min="1" max="1">halt_baddata</stringmatch>
</decompilertest>
